. 
. * ============================
. * Analysis
. * ============================
. 
. * HYPOTHESIS TEST FOR H3
. 
.         * For consistency of approach between all different hypotheses and gi
> ven data
.         * availability of outcome variables (in particular of gender attitude
> s for H1
.         * and H4), we use a cross-sectional approach (OLS w/fixed effects) ex
> ploiting the
.         * random assignment of gender of the first child, conditional on beco
> ming a parent.
.         * Hence, we prioritize causality over perfect adherence to the hypoth
> esis, and we
.         * test the effect of having a daughter instead of a son as a first ch
> ild.
.         * See the PAP for a further discussion on the empirical approach.
.         
.         * In our main specification, we only include pre-treatment controls
.         * (i.e., sex of the parent and years since the first child was born +
>  FEs for 
.         * birth region and age of the parent and survey year).
.         * Accordingly, we run a linear OLS regression analysis with fixed eff
> ects with a
.         * measure of female share of weekly housework (generated using pli004
> 3_h, pli0012_h, 
.         * and pli0016_h)in opposite-sex couples (identified using pid and par
> id) as dependent 
.         * variable  and the dummy variable first_child_daughter as independen
> t variable. 
.         * We do not track partners over time by creating permanent couple IDs
> , but we 
.         * only focus on partners identified for each specific survey year. Th
> is choice is to
.         * keep the focus on individuals as in the other hypotheses testings, 
> and we can do so
.         * because the treatment is permanent (i.e., once an individual become
> s parent of a daughter
.         * as a first child, our independent variable becomes 1 and is carried
>  over to any new
.         * couples that the individual enters or exites).
.         * See the PAP for a further discussion on the empirical approach.
. 
